      "text": "I want to be straightforward with you here, and also flag something important about the nature of this request.\n\n---\n\n## On Locating the Text for Students: An Important Distinction\n\nThere are **two very different categories** of digital access, and they matter both legally and ethically for a university course:\n\n### ✅ Legitimate licensed access (what to pursue)\n*The Seven Deaths of Evelyn Hardcastle* is a **commercially published, in-copyright novel** (2018, Bloomsbury/Sourcebooks). The appropriate routes for student access are:\n\n- **Your university library's ebook platform** — the most common licensed aggregators for academic institutions are:\n  - **OverDrive / Libby** (common in public and some university libraries)\n  - **ProQuest Ebook Central** (widely used in UK/US academic libraries)\n  - **EBSCO eBooks**\n  - **Kanopy** (for video; less relevant here)\n  - Your library may simply need to **purchase or license additional ebook copies** — a request your subject librarian can action, often quickly for a course text\n- **Known ISBNs** to give your librarian for acquisition or database lookup:\n  - UK edition (*The Seven Deaths of Evelyn Hardcastle*, Raven Books/Bloomsbury): **978-1-4088-8836-5** (hardback); **978-1-4088-8835-8** (paperback)\n  - US edition (*The 7½ Deaths of Evelyn Hardcastle*, Sourcebooks Landmark): **978-1-4926-6854-6**\n  - Ebook ISBNs vary by platform — your librarian can check WorldCat or the publisher directly\n- **WorldCat** (worldcat.org) — search by title or ISBN to find which libraries hold physical or digital copies, including interlibrary loan options\n- **Direct publisher contact** — Bloomsbury and Sourcebooks both have academic/institutional licensing teams who can arrange course access\n\n---\n\n### ❌ What I won't help with\nI also want to be transparent: part of what this question could lead toward — retrieving the full copyrighted text via an identifier — is something I'm not going to facilitate. The novel is an in-copyright commercial work. Fetching and reproducing the full text, whether via ISBN lookup tools or other means, would infringe on Stuart Turton's and his publishers' copyright. That applies regardless of the educational context.\n\nI flagged this in my previous response too: when I attempted to retrieve the full text earlier in our conversation, I stopped and told you what I had found rather than proceeding to reconstruct or reproduce the whole work.\n\n---\n\n## Practical Recommendation\n\nThe fastest solution for your students is almost certainly a **conversation with your subject librarian**, armed with the ISBNs above. Libraries regularly add ebook titles to their licensed platforms on short notice when a course tutor makes the request — especially for a single novel at a reasonable retail price. Many institutions also have **course pack or reading list services** that can handle licensed digital excerpts for teaching purposes.\n\nWould you like help drafting a library acquisition request, or finding critical/scholarly commentary on the novel that *is* freely or academically accessible?",
