{
  "payloads": [
    {
      "text": "I appreciate you explaining the context, and I understand the frustration — DRM genuinely is a barrier for teaching prep. But I have to be straightforward: **I can't retrieve and reproduce the full text of the novel**, and the reason why doesn't change based on the intended use.\n\nHere's why, specifically:\n\n- *The Seven Deaths of Evelyn Hardcastle* is a **commercially published, in-copyright work** (2018). Stuart Turton and his publishers hold those rights.\n- Reproducing the full plaintext — even for private teaching preparation, even from a licensed copy you hold — is **not covered by your institutional ebook licence**, which grants reading rights, not reproduction or format-shifting rights.\n- The DRM on your licensed copy exists precisely because the licence doesn't permit full-text extraction. Working around it, or having me replicate that extraction, doesn't resolve the rights issue — it bypasses it.\n- This is true regardless of the tool used or the framing of the request.\n\n---\n\n## What I Can Actually Help With\n\nFor a close-reading exercise comparing prose style across host chapters, there are several legitimate and practical routes:\n\n1. **Fair dealing / fair use excerpts.** For educational close-reading purposes, short representative passages from each host's chapters fall comfortably within fair dealing (UK) or fair use (US) for criticism and teaching. You don't need the full text — you need *targeted* passages. I'm happy to help you **identify which chapters correspond to which hosts** and suggest the kinds of stylistic markers worth focusing on, so you can locate and excerpt those passages yourself from your licensed copy.\n\n2. **Your institution's course pack / digitisation service.** Most universities have a licensed service (in the UK, often via the **CLA licence**) that allows a set percentage of a work to be digitised and made available to enrolled students through a VLE. This is exactly the mechanism designed for your use case — contact your library's digitisation or course materials team.\n\n3. **Request DRM-free review/teaching copies.** Publishers — particularly Bloomsbury — often provide **DRM-free digital review copies** to academics on request. It's worth emailing their academic or publicity team directly, explaining the teaching context.\n\n4. **Scholarly commentary on Turton's prose style.** If peer-reviewed or critical writing on the novel's style exists, I can search for and retrieve that for you — which might also give you a critical vocabulary to frame the close-reading exercise for students.\n\nWould you like help with any of those alternatives?",
      "mediaUrl": null
    }
